[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66308,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovarian dysgenesis includes congenital disorders of sex development, with increased risk of developing gonadoblastoma and other germ cell tumours.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66309,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "<h5><strong>MIM numbering</strong></h5>\r\n<p>136435 Follicle-stimulating hormone receptor; FSHR</p>\r\n<p>300697 HECT, UBA, and WWE domains-containing protein&nbsp;1; HUWE1</p>\r\n<p>480000 Sex-determining region Y; SRY</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:45:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66310,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LD2A Malformative disorders of sex development</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66311,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> disorders of sex development; complete (pure) gonadal dysgenesis; partial (mixed) gonadal dysgenesis; Turner syndrome; Swyer syndrome</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66312,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis (Swyer syndrome); 46,XX complete gonadal dysgenesis; 45,X complete gonadal dysgenesis (Turner syndrome and variants); partial gonadal dysgenesis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66313,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovarian fossa</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66314,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Individuals with complete gonadal dysgenesis (CGD) usually present with primary amenorrhoea and absence of secondary sexual characteristics, whereas those with partial gonadal dysgenesis (PGD) usually present with ambiguous genitalia at birth. The degree of genital ambiguity ranges widely, from an almost female phenotype with clitoromegaly at one extreme to an almost male phenotype with isolated hypospadias at the other. There is susceptibility to gonadoblastoma and germinoma {31244770; 30753444}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66315,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The epidemiology is unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66316,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In 46,XX CGD, inactivating mutations of <em>FSHR</em> (2p21-p16) have been described {20374735}. In 46,XY CGD (Swyer syndrome), mutations and deletions in <em>SRY</em> account for 10&ndash;20% of cases, and alterations have also been reported in many other genes involved in sex determination, including <em>SOX9</em>, <em>WT1</em>, <em>DHH</em>, <em>GATA4</em>, <em>NR5A1</em>, <em>NR0B1</em> (<em>DAX1</em>), <em>DMRT1</em>, <em>CBX2</em>, <em>ATRX</em>, <em>MAP3K1</em>, and <em>FGF9</em> {30550360; 31446095}. 45,X CGD is caused by partial or complete loss of an X&nbsp;chromosome. The cause and pathogenesis of gonadal dysgenesis at the molecular level remain largely unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66317,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gonadoblastoma candidate genes, <em>DDX3Y</em> and <em>TSPY1</em>, were postulated to be present on the proximal part of the short and long arms of the Y&nbsp;chromosome. <em>DDX3Y</em>-positive and <em>TSPY1</em>-positive gonadal dysgenesis has the highest tumour risk for gonadoblastoma and germinoma {31244770; 30753444}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66318,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In CGD, there is rudimentary or invisible gonadal tissue in both ovarian fossae. In PGD, there is a normally developed testis covered with tunica albuginea on one side and rudimentary or maldeveloped gonadal tissue on the other side. Tumours developing in this syndrome show macroscopic changes similar to those of their non-syndromic counterparts.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66319,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Bilateral streak gonads in CGD are almost exclusively composed of ovarian-type stroma and rarely contain sex cord&ndash;like structures. In PGD, there is normally developed testicular tissue with well-formed seminiferous tubules on one side and a streak gonad on the other side; the streak gonad has a variety of histological features, including trabeculae or cord-like structures consisting of an intimate mixture of primitive germ cells and sex cord&ndash;like cells (or composed only of stromal cells) or only a few germ cells in the ovarian-type stroma {31721911}. Tumours that develop in this syndrome have no histological features distinct from those of their non-syndromic counterparts.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-10T10:00:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66320,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66321,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66322,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rudimentary tissue composed of ovarian-type stroma without any well-developed follicular structure or with primitive sex cord&ndash;like structures is the diagnostic clue for the dysgenetic gonad.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66323,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovarian dysgenesis is staged according to the tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66324,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 471,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:44:53"
      }
